Let $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ be vectors such that $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\| = 3$ and $\\|\\mathbf{w}\\| = 4.$  Find the minimum value of $\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{w}.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"d73ddb4210798edeb1b394ea09458ab13a98b85ab29236b9f5fd5e0e839ad41c"},"seconds":7.444936037063599,"seed":435253720115260524,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the minimum value of the dot product \\(\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{w}\\) given that \\(\\|\\mathbf{v}\\| = 3\\) and \\(\\|\\mathbf{w}\\| = 4\\), we start by recalling the formula for the dot product in terms of the magnitudes of the vectors and the cosine of the angle \\(\\theta\\) between them:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{w} = \\|\\mathbf{v}\\| \\|\\mathbf{w}\\| \\cos \\theta.\n\\]\nSubstituting the given magnitudes, we have:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{w} = 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot \\cos \\theta = 12 \\cos \\theta.\n\\]\nThe cosine of the angle \\(\\theta\\) ranges from \\(-1\\) to \\(1\\). Therefore, the minimum value of \\(\\cos \\theta\\) is \\(-1\\). Substituting \\(\\cos \\theta = -1\\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{w} = 12 \\cdot (-1) = -12.\n\\]\nThus, the minimum value of \\(\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{w}\\) is \\(\\boxed{-12}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{-12}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"precalculus:540"}
